t(10, 655, """The level of social justice in [China] today is marked by a great paradox: while the state has succeeded in making unprecedented historic achievements in eliminating extreme poverty, it at the same time faces a sharp and growing gap in the distribution of wealth and income between the rich and the poor.

The Chinese model is currently going through a stage of comprehensive reassessment under the slogan of "common prosperity" launched by President Xi Jinping to curb unrestrained capitalism and narrow class gaps.
The reality of social justice in China today can be summarised through the following main axes:
1. The greatest achievement: eradicating extreme poverty
China records a huge point of superiority in the social protection file; it has officially succeeded in lifting more than 850 million citizens out of extreme poverty over the past decades. The poorest half of China's population now owns assets and social protection far better than corresponding groups in major capitalist countries.
2. The widening wealth gap (the Gini coefficient)
Despite the elimination of poverty, the concentration of wealth in the hands of the elite has seen a big leap; independent economic studies (such as studies of the Prosperity Institute at Zhejiang University) indicate that the Gini coefficient for wealth in China has passed the 0.7 barrier (where the number 1 expresses absolute inequality), and the top group (1% of the population) holds a share of personal wealth exceeding what the poorest half of society owns by about five times.
3. The structural gap between countryside and city (the Hukou system)
The geographic gap between residents of thriving coastal cities and residents of the inland countryside is one of the deepest challenges to social justice. This goes back historically to the residence registration system, which ties receiving basic services (such as high-quality free education, advanced health care and pensions) to the citizen's original place of birth. Despite gradual reforms, migrant workers inside Chinese cities still face difficulties in getting the same privileges obtained by the cities' original residents.
4. Real estate as an obstacle to social justice
The huge real-estate boom in past decades caused housing to turn into a main tool of speculation and wealth accumulation instead of being a social benefit; inequality in home ownership contributes about 75% of the total wealth gap among citizens. This inflation has made owning flats in major cities an almost impossible dream for young generations.
5. Unemployment pressures among youth and the "lying flat" movement
Social justice faces a critical challenge represented by the rise of the unemployment rate among urban youth (age group 16-24) to record levels, which has led to a state of social frustration and the birth of silent protest and cultural movements among youth, such as the culture of "Tang Ping" (lying flat) and "Bai Lan" (letting it rot), which express young people's rejection of the exhausting work culture amid declining opportunities for fair social mobility and limited returns compared with the effort made.
## The government's current direction:
Beijing fully realises that sharp class disparity threatens political stability and sustainable growth; so the state today adopts strict measures including:
* Campaigns to curb the technology, gaming and private tutoring sectors to ease the financial burdens on families.
* Imposing restrictions on public displays of extreme wealth, and awareness of fighting corruption and avoiding showing off wealth on social media.
* The rural revitalisation strategy to direct investments and services toward less developed regions.""")

h(11, 300, """Did China leap?""")

t(11, 825, """The "Boxer Rebellion", which broke out between 1899 and 1901, is one of the strangest tragedies of history, in which angry popular despair blended with spiritual superstition in a direct confrontation with modern military technology.

1. Who are the "Boxers"? 🥊

The movement began with poor peasants in northern China who suffered from a harsh drought and a fierce famine, and they poured their anger on foreigners and Christian missionaries, whom they considered a cause of the gods' wrath and the destruction of the country's culture.

These founded a secret society called the "Society of Righteous and Harmonious Fists" (Yihequan).

The Westerners gave them the name "Boxers" because they saw them practising movement rituals resembling boxing and traditional martial arts (kung fu).

2. Magic and superstition against bullets and artillery 🔮🛡️

What distinguishes this rebellion is the "Boxers'" absolute faith in the spiritual and magical support they thought would protect them from modern Western weapons. Their beliefs and rituals included the following:

Armour of immunity from bullets: the Boxers would enter a state of spiritual trance or hypnosis through Sufi-like religious rituals, believing that the spirits of ancestors and gods possessed their bodies. They firmly believed that these rituals made their bodies immune to bullets and artillery shells, and that the Westerners' bullets would scatter or turn to water merely by touching their bodies!

Fighting technology with magic: they believed that Western machines such as "steam trains" and "telegraph lines" were satanic innovations that cut the natural energy of the earth (feng shui). They thought they could destroy these machines and defeat the Western armies using only magic and martial arts, without needing modern rifles.

3. The horrifying shock and the suicidal reality 💣

When the real confrontations began, the Boxers' superstitions collided with the bitter and violent technological reality:

Facing machine guns: thousands of Boxers, wearing traditional red and white clothes and carrying swords, spears and sticks, rushed toward the Western defence lines, shouting with confidence that the bullets would not harm them.

The massacre: the Westerners were armed with modern rapid-fire rifles and primitive machine guns (such as the Maxim gun). The result was horrifying massacres; the Boxers were mown down by the thousands without even managing to reach the foreign soldiers. Despite seeing their comrades fall dead, their leaders justified it by saying the victims "did not have enough faith" or "broke the magic rituals".

4. The Eight-Nation Alliance 🌍

The matter escalated when the Boxers (with secret and hesitant support from the Chinese Empress "Cixi") besieged""")

t(12, 60, """the diplomatic quarter in Beijing, in which the foreigners held out for 55 days.

In response, the "Eight-Nation Alliance" was formed (comprising Britain, Russia, Japan, France, the United States, Germany, Italy and Austria-Hungary). This alliance sent a modern and strong army that succeeded in:

Storming the capital Beijing and breaking the siege.

Crushing the rebellion with extreme brutality and wide-scale looting of the city and its palaces.

Forcing China to sign the "Boxer Protocol" in 1901, another humiliating treaty that imposed on China payment of huge financial compensations exceeding its budget for years, and acceptance of a permanent foreign military presence in its capital.

The Boxer Rebellion was a tragic proof of the huge civilisational and technological gap between East and West at that time, and it proved to the Chinese that clinging to old traditions and superstitions would not protect the country, which hastened the fall of the imperial system a few years later (in 1912) and the start of a comprehensive national modernisation movement.""")

h(12, 1100, """What happened in China?""")

t(12, 1785, """How did the opium trade destroy Chinese society? 🛑

Opium was not merely an ordinary commodity; Britain used it as a "deadly economic and social weapon" to overturn the balance of power. Here is how that happened:

The British trade deficit: at first, Britain suffered a huge trade deficit with China; the British loved Chinese tea, silk and porcelain and bought them in huge quantities. In return, China was self-sufficient, did not want to buy any European goods, and insisted on being paid in silver only. This made Britain's silver reserves leave it and pile up in China.

The wicked solution (opium): the British East India Company found the solution in growing opium (the drug) in huge quantities in India (which it occupied), then smuggling it into China through networks of corrupt smugglers, although the Chinese emperor had banned its entry.

Total addiction and collapse: opium spread like fire in dry straw, and addiction was not limited to the common people or the poor but reached soldiers, government officials and even members of the ruling family. Estimates indicate that millions of Chinese became addicts, which led to:

Paralysis of production and the army: the country's productive capacity fell, and soldiers became unable to fight because of withdrawal symptoms and addiction.""")

t(13, 60, """The reverse silver drain: the situation turned upside down completely; silver began to leave China in frightening quantities to pay the British for opium, which caused a crushing economic crisis for the Chinese Empire.

When the Chinese emperor tried to appoint a strict official named "Lin Zexu" to confiscate British opium and burn it in the sea (something like the American Boston Tea Party), Britain took this as a pretext to declare the First Opium War to protect "freedom of trade"!

How did the other Western states exploit China's weakness? 🐺

When the other colonial powers saw how Britain, with its few ships, was able to humiliate the great Chinese Empire and force it to give up its lands and silver, they realised that this "Asian giant" was in fact "the sick man of Asia", and they all rushed to get their share of the cake:

The Second Opium War (1856-1860): France joined Britain in a second war against China. This war ended in another crushing defeat for China, and British and French forces reached the capital Beijing and burned and looted the Imperial Summer Palace (which was considered a priceless architectural and artistic masterpiece).

Treaties of submission and the partition of China: China was forced to sign new treaties (the treaties of Tianjin and Beijing) that were not limited to Britain and France; the United States and the Russian Empire also entered the line.

Dividing China into "spheres of influence": these states did not occupy China completely as a single state (because its area is very huge and hard to administer); rather they divided it into economic and military spheres of influence:

Russia: seized vast lands in north-east China (the Manchuria region and coasts overlooking the Pacific).

France: controlled areas in southern China near its colonies in Vietnam (Burjuwan).

Germany: occupied the Kiautschou region and the Shandong peninsula.

Japan (the biggest shock): Japan, the Asian neighbour that China historically considered smaller and of less worth, modernised its army in the Western way, defeated China in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894-1895), and seized the island of Taiwan.

The Open Door Policy (Open Door Policy): the United States feared the European states would divide China completely and close the markets to it, so it imposed a policy guaranteeing all Western states an equal right to exploit ports and trade inside China, which made China almost completely open to the West without having real sovereignty over its lands and ports.

This chain of defeats and humiliation generated huge Chinese popular anger that later led to great revolutions (such as the Boxer Rebellion) and ended in the complete fall of the Chinese imperial system in 1912, paving the way for decades of civil wars until the rise of the Communist Party and the founding of the modern China we know today, which is still governed by the complex of history and wants to prove that it will not be defeated or humiliated again by the West.""")

h(13, 2200, """China's complex""")

t(14, 345, """The First World War was a war of duty, and it saw wide-scale volunteering before the shock of the trenches struck thousands of soldiers on the fronts, so that the war turned for the soldier into merely an imposed war from which he had to try to escape with his life. Then the Second World War came and became an ideological war for the soldiers of both sides, as the war became for the soldier a war of existence and a war to protect what he thought was a life heritage, while the propaganda on both sides worked to frighten societies and tell them that the other side wanted to destroy your way of life!

Yet, because Western societies are not originally societies of wars, the capitals of both the Allied and Axis sides saw wide popular demonstrations demanding an end to the war after the weight of the bill became clear to people.

Three or four years ago I wrote that Western and non-Western societies, the world as a whole, are not prepared for a long war because of the change in the geo-psychological landscape of nations and peoples due to the effect of globalisation in the last 30 years.

The world no longer believes in ideology except for a small few of the Muslims who may represent a third of the Islamic Ummah, and Allah knows best.

The Westerner and the Easterner today are both directed by the media, deceived by what the media of each side says. And if the ideology that marked the mind and thinking of the soldier of the Second World War, not the First, has died, then ideology will not be present today in the Third World War, just as in the First World War, where there was no ideology either.

And the map became as follows:
First world war: no religious ideology, only the call of duty to defend the homeland, and masses very eager to join the fighting before the shock of the trenches happened, that shock which made them regret their enthusiasm for the war at its start.
Second world war: a human intellectual, not religious, ideology, a war of existence, and the soldier's insistence on fighting the fight of a worldly, life-centred intellectual creed (ideology).
Third world war: no religious and no human intellectual ideology, only a call of grudging duty, severe distress in the Western and Eastern soldier, and going out to fight without psychological readiness; yet the media machine on both sides will blot out this truth to show the opposite and to manipulate the minds of peoples as much as possible.
Fourth and last world war: a massive religious ideology between the two sides (the Muslims and the Europeans).""")

h(14, 2000, """Ideology dies
but armies do not care""")

t(15, 105, """Talk of the two testimonies of faith being written on the banners of the Prophet ﷺ relies on weak historical narrations that the hadith scholars criticised precisely, while the authentic hadiths describe only the colours, with no mention of writing.

1. The weak narrations about writing the two testimonies and the scholars' criticism of them

Hadiths have come mentioning the writing of the two testimonies, but they are all defective and very weak in their chains, the most prominent being:

The narration of al-Tabarani in al-Mu'jam al-Awsat and al-Saghir: from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: "The banner of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was black and his standard white, on which was written: There is no god but Allah, Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah."

The defect of the hadith: in its chain is a narrator called "Hayyan ibn Jabalah" (or Hayyan ibn Ubaydullah), of whom al-Hafiz Ibn Hajar said: "abandoned in hadith", and the hadith scholars weakened him in detail, and there is consensus that the hadith is weak and objectionable.

The narration of Abu al-Shaykh in the book "Akhlaq al-Nabi ﷺ": from the hadith of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him: "The standard of the Prophet ﷺ was white, with written on it: There is no god but Allah, Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah."

The defect of the hadith: in its chain is a narrator called "Muhammad ibn Yunus al-Kudaymi", who is accused of fabrication and lying by the masters among the hadith scholars, so the hadith was judged fabricated or severely objectionable.

2. The established authentic hadiths (plain banners)

The hadiths that described the banners of the Prophet ﷺ in the books of Sahih and Sunan (such as Sahih al-Bukhari, Sunan Abi Dawud and Sunan al-Tirmidhi) came clear in describing only the colours and fabrics, and did not mention a single letter written on them, including:

The hadith of Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her: from Amr ibn Ali, from Ibn Abi Adi, from Shu'bah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Bakhtari, from Aisha: "The banner of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was black, of a striped woollen cloth" (narrated by al-Tirmidhi, who said: hasan gharib). The mirt murahhal is a woollen cloth with a light pattern like the lines of camel saddles, and it is a plain fabric with nothing written on it.

The hadith of al-Bara' ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with him: when asked about the banner of the Prophet ﷺ he said: "It was black, square, of namirah" (narrated by Abu Dawud and al-Tirmidhi). Namirah is a fabric with white and black in it (like a leopard's skin), and it too was plain.

The hadith of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him: "The Prophet ﷺ entered Makkah and his standard was white" (narrated by Abu Dawud, al-Tirmidhi and Ibn Majah). No writing is mentioned in it.

3. The military and practical reason for plain banners

The jurists and historians explained the banners being free of writing by several practical and military reasons:

The function of the military banner: the first and basic purpose of the banner in ancient wars is direction and distinction. The army is divided into a right wing, a left wing and a centre, and each brigade and tribe has a colour by which they know their position and their commander's position amid the dust and fighting. Putting complex words and phrases that cannot be read from far distances makes the banner lose its visual function.

The banner being exposed to tearing and falling: the banner in battle is always targeted by enemies to bring it down (as happened in the Battle of Mu'tah). And the Muslims know the banner may fall to the ground, be trampled underfoot or torn by enemies, so it is not logical in Sharia or reason to write the holiest words (the name of Allah and His Messenger) on something exposed to direct desecration in fierce fighting.

Based on this verification, attaching the slogan of "the two testimonies written on the banner" to the Prophetic era is a historically late innovation; some Islamic states in late and modern eras adopted writing verses or the two testimonies on their flags as a kind of political and religious slogan, but it is not a reported Prophetic Sunnah as people think.""")

h(16, 250, """After a conscript trampled
a flag with the two testimonies written on it
The Sharia position""")

h(16, 1100, """O Allah, send down Your command
O Generous One, the span of the distresses has grown long, their magnitude has become severe, and their succession has increased""")

t(16, 1790, """And that will be, God willing, in the years before the descent of the Prophet of Allah Isa, peace be upon him; and when that happens, the fear for provision dies in people's souls, so they turn to that for which they were created, which is "worship"; their hearts are joined, rancour and envy are pulled from their chests, and people become equal, so there is no poor and rich, but like the teeth of a comb; the mosques fill with men and women who remember Allah, hearts fill with richness and contentment, the grudges between fathers and sons vanish, and "the majority, and Allah knows best" submit to the command of Allah and their necks bow to their Lord. And people enter the religion of Allah the Exalted in crowds.""")

h(16, 2300, """The Muslims at the end of the Ummah's term:
homes given into ownership, free food supplies, clean infrastructure, and wealth that overflows without running out""")

t(17, 545, """Because before the descent of the Prophet of Allah Isa at the end of time, an Ummah arises on an earth unlike our earth today, so everything is different; everything people today see as an utmost necessity dies! And all the habits, ideas and order of post-1945 are wiped out, and a mental, psychological and intellectual compulsion arises that is merciless, overpowering and of tremendous grip. All the sheep return to the fold, willingly and unwillingly. And when the door of the fold closes and the wolves remain howling in the wilderness, alone and hungry, then what people want will not be in the world, but .. only what Allah the Exalted wants""")

h(17, 1100, """The Muslims at the end of the Ummah's term:
no physics and chemistry, integration and differentiation, no investment fever, no fever of the world and matter""")

t(17, 1810, """God willing. And that will be before the descent of the Prophet of Allah Isa, peace be upon him""")

h(17, 2200, """The Muslims at the end of the Ummah's term:
they will go by the millions to Sharia knowledge, and the mosques will not close at night because of the many who stand in prayer""")
